<?xml version="1.0"?>

<!--
  Mi-17 main rotor drive, side 1 of 2: one TV3-117MT turboshaft,
  combined with mi17_main_engine_2 through <gearbox name="mi17-main-
  gearbox"> in mi17.xml (this branch's FGGearbox, feature/REQ-003-
  fggearbox) into the single main rotor shaft.

  Now on <turboshaft_engine> (FGTurboshaft, src/models/propulsion/
  FGTurboshaft.{h,cpp}), not <turboprop_engine> (FGTurboProp): same
  thermodynamics/tables (FGTurboshaft is a derivative of FGTurboProp
  with the propeller/beta-range/reverse-pitch handling removed, since
  a turboshaft drives a gearbox/rotor, not a variable-pitch prop), but
  with a real, script-settable starter-cmd property added. Without
  that, the only way to start an FGTurboProp engine from a script is
  propulsion/set-running, which forces full throttle directly
  (FGPropulsion::SetEngineRunning()) and converges to a steady state
  through FGPropulsion::GetSteadyState()'s internal iteration loop
  inside a single simulation frame; for an aircraft with an RPM
  governor moderating throttle in response to shaft RPM, that produces
  a large, unphysical one-frame RPM overshoot (confirmed via this
  aircraft's own ground-run scripts: shaft RPM spiking to ~1.7x the
  governed target before settling back down), because the governor
  never gets a chance to react during that synthetic convergence.
  starter-cmd drives the real tsOff->tsSpinUp->tsStart->tsRun phase
  machine on the real simulation dt instead, so the governor's normal
  throttle output is what the engine actually sees throughout; see
  scripts/test_mi17_ground_run_idle.xml for the updated start sequence
  that exercises this.

  This is the same per-side derivation once prototyped on the sibling
  feature/turboshaft branch's mi17_main_engine.xml (<twin_turboshaft_
  engine>, which models both sides internally in one combined class);
  that approach was not used here; FGTurboshaft keeps this file's own
  established per-side-engine-plus-FGGearbox architecture (feature/
  REQ-003-fggearbox) intact rather than replacing it with a different
  twin-engine design.

  maxpower=2200 HP/side (4400 HP combined): real TV3-117VM MAX RATED
  shaft horsepower, W/O PSS column (particle separator system isn't
  modeled here), from the type's performance table (Table 4.2). Was
  1000 HP/side, an arbitrary placeholder ("not sourced from a real
  TV3-117MT datasheet", per this file's own prior comment) that turned
  out to be the actual reason the aircraft could not generate enough
  thrust to lift off at all: EnginePowerRPM_N1's cell values already
  reach up to ~2124 HP (see FGTurboProp::Calculate(): EngPower_HP is
  read from the table, THEN clamped to MaxPower), so the 1000 HP ceiling
  was silently cutting delivered power by more than half regardless of
  what the table's own shape allowed.
  EnginePowerRPM_N1's RPM axis is scaled from this model's own
  transmission (mi17_main_rotor.xml gearratio=78.125 x nominalrpm=192 =
  15000 EngineRPM at 100% N1, matching the real Mi-17's ~15000 RPM
  engine-to-gearbox input speed), not copied from the AH-1S/M601-style
  turboprop_example's propeller-shaft-scale RPM axis.
-->
<turboshaft_engine name="mi17_main_engine_1">
  <idlen1>                       60.0   </idlen1>
  <maxn1>                       100.0   </maxn1>
  <maxpower unit="HP">         2200.0   </maxpower>
  <psfc unit="LBS/HR/HP">         0.55  </psfc>
  <n1idle_max_delay>              1     </n1idle_max_delay>
  <maxstartingtime>              20     </maxstartingtime>
  <startern1>                    20     </startern1>
  <!-- ielumaxtorque intentionally omitted (disables the IELU torque
       limiter; FGTurboProp default is -1/disabled). 2100 FT*LB was
       calibrated against nominal-RPM torque, but torque = power/omega
       spikes enormously at the very low RPM seen during startup (a
       near-stalled shaft needs huge torque to make any power at all;
       physically real, not a bug), so it was tripping on the ordinary
       startup transient and choking throttle back to near zero before
       the real run even began (confirmed via temporary diagnostics:
       Ielu_intervent=1 and ThrottlePos ramping down from 1.0 within a
       few GetSteadyState() convergence iterations). A properly-tuned
       overtorque protection curve (limiting by RPM band, not a single
       constant) is follow-up work, not done here. -->
  <itt_delay>                     0.05  </itt_delay>

  <table name="EnginePowerVC">
    <description> Engine power, function of airspeed and pressure </description>
    <independentVar lookup="row">atmosphere/P-psf</independentVar>
    <independentVar lookup="column">velocities/ve-kts</independentVar>
    <tableData>
                 0     50    100    150    200    250
       503   0.357  0.380  0.400  0.425  0.457  0.486
      1048   0.586  0.589  0.600  0.621  0.650  0.686
      1328   0.707  0.721  0.731  0.757  0.786  0.821
      1496   0.779  0.786  0.808  0.821  0.857  0.900
      1684   0.850  0.857  0.874  0.900  0.943  0.979
      1896   0.914  0.929  0.946  0.971  1      1.057
      2135   1      1.011  1.029  1.043  1.083  1.150
      2213   1.029  1.043  1.057  1.079  1.114  1.171
    </tableData>
  </table>

  <table name="EnginePowerRPM_N1" type="internal">
    <description>
      Per-side engine power [HP], function of EngineRPM (rotor RPM x
      gearratio 78.125) and N1 [%]. Nominal main-rotor speed (192 RPM)
      corresponds to EngineRPM 15000, between the 14114 and 15616 rows.

      Row axis rescaled by 15000/4995 (=1000/333) from the original
      0/1900/2850/3800/4700/5200 breakpoints when gearratio/nominalrpm
      were changed from the placeholder 18.5/270 to the real Mi-17
      78.125/192 (see mi17_main_rotor.xml); same relative shape,
      stretched to match the new EngineRPM scale.

      Each row here is the original (undocumented-source) reference
      shape scaled by min(1, RPM/15000); i.e. available power now
      scales down toward zero as RPM drops, not just as N1 drops.
      Without this, the table let a near-stalled shaft (RPM close to
      0) draw close to full rated power at high N1, and since
      torque = power/omega diverges as omega -> 0, that produced
      torque spikes of tens of thousands of FT*LB during startup;
      confirmed via temporary diagnostics (FGRotor's shaft RPM
      integrator overshooting straight to its MaximalRPM clamp within
      a single GetSteadyState() convergence step). Real turboshafts
      are torque-limited during spool-up specifically to avoid this;
      this table now approximates that instead of relying on IELU (a
      single fixed torque threshold) to catch it after the fact.
    </description>
    <tableData>
              0     5     60     86     94     95     96     97     98     99    100    101
         0    0   1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4    1.4
      5706    0   0.5     38    217    380    413    456    500    544    576    630    674
      8559    0   0.8     49    375    652    701    766    823    896    962   1044   1109
     11411    0   1.1     11    522    956   1032   1108   1195   1293   1369   1478   1565
     14114    0   0.3    0.3    605   1210   1330   1411   1519   1640   1747   1882   2003
     15616    0   0.3    0.3    586   1257   1371   1471   1600   1729   1843   1986   2124
    </tableData>
  </table>

  <table name="ITT_N1" type="internal">
    <description> Inter-Turbine Temperature ITT [deg C] depending on N1 and engine run (0=off / 1=running) </description>
    <tableData>
              0     1
        0     0     0
       15   100   100
       60   180   520
       96   270   680
      100   280   730
    </tableData>
  </table>

</turboshaft_engine>
